        "why": "An operon is a SWITCH, so the drawing has to show both positions of the switch; one panel shows a gene map, two panels show how it is regulated.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Drawing one panel only. The question is about regulation, so both the repressed and the induced state are needed.",
          "Putting the genes in the wrong order. It is i, p, o, z, y, a — regulator, promoter, operator, then the three structural genes.",
          "Labelling both panels 'absence of inducer'. The guide does exactly this; the second panel is the PRESENCE of inducer."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Two panels: repressor on the operator, then repressor knocked off it."
      },
      {
        "id": "s2_checklist",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "What the examiner checks",
        "marks": 0,
        "margin_note": "No marks here — this is the checklist to run over your own drawing before moving on.",
        "lines": [
          "The labels the drawing must carry:",
          "i = regulator gene   p = promoter",
          "o = operator         z, y, a = structural genes",
          "z codes β-galactosidase, y codes permease,",
          "a codes transacetylase.",
          "Panel 1: repressor bound to the operator, so",
          "RNA polymerase cannot transcribe.",
          "Panel 2: inducer (lactose) binds the repressor,",
          "the repressor is inactivated, lac mRNA is made."
        ],
        "why": "Writing which enzyme each structural gene codes for costs three lines and answers the follow-up question examiners most often attach to this figure.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Labelling the boxes but not saying what the letters mean. The key is part of the labelling mark.",
          "Saying the inducer binds the operator. The inducer binds the REPRESSOR."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "The inducer works on the repressor, never on the DNA."
      }
